One common practice to solve this problem is to use a configuration/settings object, that just stores all your settings properties. Pass this object in to your advanced configuration form, and the form can directly update the properties of the object without needing any return value. 

So for instance you'd define a class like:
```csharp
public class AdvancedConfig
{
    public bool CheckBox1Enabled { get; set; }
    public string ComboBox1Selection { get; set; }

    // ... etc for all your forms settings
}
```
Then your form's constructor might look like:
```csharp
public AdvancedForm(AdvancedConfig config)
{
    InitializeComponent();

    // Do assignment to checkboxes/combos with config values, for example:
    checkbox1.Checked = config.CheckBox1Enabled;
    comboBox1.Text = config.ComboBox1Selection;
    // etc... 
}
```
And back in your main form you'd do:
```csharp
// Create a config object somehow
AdvancedConfig config = new AdvancedConfig { 
    CheckBox1Enabled = true, 
    ComboBox1Selection = "SomeValue" 
};

// Pass it to the form
AdvancedForm form = new AdvancedForm(config);

// Now, the form can update the config object directly
form.ShowDialog();
```
The advantage is that you're not dealing with a variable number of arguments, and you can keep adding more properties to your settings object without having to change constructors. As a bonus, just from looking at this object, another developer can quickly see what parameters are associated with your form without having to navigate through multiple methods. 

This method especially shines when you need to parse and save configurations, like from/to JSON or XML. It's easier to serialize one, well-structured object than parse multiple fields from a dictionary.

However, is important to make sure this object doesn't become a 'god' object with hundreds of properties. You should pay attention to class cohesion and try to group related properties into separate classes where appropriate, and perhaps use composition to structure them in the main class.

Designing a system to sort a list of numbers in Java can be achieved by implementing any sorting algorithm, such as quicksort, insertion sort, selection sort, etc. Here is a simple system using Bubble Sort algorithm which is easy to understand, but it's not the most efficient one.

Below is a java class for a system that sorts a list of numbers using Bubble Sort:

```java
public class NumberSorter {

    /*
     * Bubble Sort algorithm
     */
    public void bubbleSort(int[] numbers) {
        int n = numbers.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                if (numbers[j] > numbers[j + 1]) {
                    //swap numbers[j] and numbers[j+1]
                    int temp = numbers[j];
                    numbers[j] = numbers[j + 1];
                    numbers[j + 1] = temp;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    public void printNumbers(int[] numbers) {
        int n = numbers.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            System.out.print(numbers[i] + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NumberSorter ns = new NumberSorter();
        int[] numbers = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
        ns.bubbleSort(numbers);
        System.out.println("Sorted array:");
        ns.printNumbers(numbers);
    }
}
```
In this system, the Bubble Sort algorithm is implemented in the `bubbleSort` method. This method sorts the numbers by repeatedly swapping the adjacent numbers if they are in wrong order. The main method tests the system by sorting an array of integers.

Please note that in real-world scenario, you would likely use built-in sorting methods from Java Collections Framework (such as `Arrays.sort()` or `Collections.sort()`) which are quite optimized. The above sample is to demonstrate how one could design a simple system for academic/learning purposes.

